Hi Vanessa and Brandon,

Intake still runs from 12:00 to 13:00 PST while the reviewer is at lunch. On =
December 5, 18 of the 32 items that need review arrive in that hour. Across a=
ll three days, dropping the lunch arrivals would leave us with 48 of the 90 r=
eview items.

Could you run a second pass with all 600 arrivals at the same times and in th=
e same order? Keep one reviewer and three minutes per item. Pause review from=
 noon until 13:00, let the arrivals queue, and have the reviewer take the old=
est waiting item at 13:00. Could you send back the open count at restart for =
each day? Please keep the item picked up at 13:00 in that count until it comp=
letes.

I've attached my comments on the staffing draft and the arrival counts by shi=
ft window. The daily minutes in the draft tell us how much review work there =
is over the shift. I'd like to see the restart counts before we use those min=
utes to discuss coverage.

Thanks,
Emily

On Tue, Dec 3, 2024 at 2:06 PM PST, Vanessa Nguyen <vanessa@mercer-document.e=
xample> wrote:

> Let's each replay the December 4=E2=80=936 lab intake using the same arriva=
l order. The attached design gives us 200 positions per day. Keep the arrival=
s that don't require review in the sequence, and keep the design's order when=
 two arrivals share a minute.
>
> Could we compare our runs Monday morning, December 9? I'd start by matching=
 the key, arrival time and review flag at each of the 600 positions. Once tho=
se match, we can compare starts and completions for the 90 review items and c=
heck what's still open at 17:00 PST on each day.
>
> Emily, could you read the attached timing definitions alongside the design?=
 I'd like a second look at where we record arrival, when review starts, and w=
hen an item comes off the open count. In particular, check that we're countin=
g an item that's already in review until it completes.

